# Logic-Based AI Under Uncertainty

## Problem: Limitations of Logic-Based Systems

* Logic-Based AI Under Uncertainty: Problem

- @Definition@: **Logic-based AI systems**:
  - Are based on propositional logic
  - Represent actions using rules
    - E.g., _"If preconditions P hold, then action A causes effect E"_

- @Example@:
  - _"If I turn the car key, the engine starts"_
  - But the battery might be dead, there's no fuel, the starter is broken, ..

- @Problem@: Real-world agents face _uncertainty_ from:
  1. _Partial observability_
     - Agent can't see the full state of the world
  2. _Non-determinism_
     - Actions don't always have deterministic or predictable outcomes
  3. _Adversarial conditions_
     - Other agents may interfere

* Logic-Based Under Uncertainty: Naive Solution

- @Naive Solution@: A possible solution to logic-based AI under uncertainty is
  to
  use:
  1. **Belief state**, i.e., set of all possible current world states
     - _Cons_: difficult to enumerate and track
  2. **Causal and exhaustive augmentation** for rules
     - _Cons_: Must consider all possible preconditions for acting, even
       unlikely
       ones
  3. **Contingent plans** to handle every possible sensor report and belief
     - _Cons_:
       - Plans become large and complex
       - No guaranteed plan may exist, yet action is required

* Naive Solution: Example of Starting a Car

- @Goal@: build a system to start a car by turning the car key

- @Remark@: Obvious preconditions
  - Car has fuel
  - Car battery is charged
  - Car key is the right one
  - ..

- @Remark@: Less obvious preconditions
  - Electrical system is intact
  - Fuel pump is working
  - Fuel lines are not clogged
  - Engine oil level is sufficient
  - ..

- @Problem@: Every precondition requires something else to achieve the goal

## Why Logic Fails Under Uncertainty

### The Qualification Problem

* Causal and Exhaustive Augmentation

- @Definition@: Causal and exhaustive augmentation of logic-rules
  - To use propositional logic under uncertainty, need to augment left-side of
    rules $X \implies Y$ to make it:
    1. _Causal_: identify true causal-effect relationships
    2. _Exhaustive_: identify all possible conditions $X$ leading to outcome $Y$
- @Limitations@:
  - Every action can have more hidden conditions needed to succeed
    - E.g., start the engine $\implies$ turn car key $\implies$ starter motor is
      working $\implies$ battery is charged $\implies$ ...
  - Preconditions can change depending on situation
    - E.g., ... $\implies$ battery is charged $\implies$ it's not too cold
      $\implies$ ...

* Limitations of Logical Qualification

- @Definition@: **Logical qualification problem** is the need to enumerate all
  preconditions necessary for an action to succeed

- @Limitations@: It is not possible to solve the logical qualification problem
  for several reasons
  1. **Laziness**: Too much effort required to create all possible rules
  2. **Theoretical ignorance**: Science doesn't always have complete theory of
     domain
     - E.g., medical science doesn't know all the "rules"
  3. **Practical ignorance**: Even if you knew all rules, you might not have all
     necessary info
     - E.g., not all required medical tests can be performed for particular
       patient

- @Consequence@: **AI winter (mid-1980s, 1990s)**
  - Researchers believed that expert systems could achieve AGI
  - Real world is complex and open-ended
  - Logical rules can't capture all necessary and sufficient conditions

### Garden World: Demonstrating the Problem

* Failure of Logic AI: Garden World Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- Consider propositions:
  - $Rain$ = _"it rains"_
  - $WetGrass$ = _"the grass is wet"_

- @Question@: Is it true that $Rain \implies WetGrass$?
  - "$Rain \implies WetGrass$" is not true in general
  - If it rains but there is a cover over grass, grass will not be wet
  - If it rains but temperature is high, wet grass might dry quickly

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}
![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L06.1.grass_sprinkler.png)
::::
:::

- @Question@: What about converse "$WetGrass \implies Rain$?
  - "$WetGrass \implies Rain$" is not true in general
    - Grass could be wet because of sprinkler system
    - Grass could be wet because of morning dew

- @Answer@: you also need to consider propositions:
  - $Cover$ = _"there is a protective cover over the grass"_
  - $Evaporate$ = _"the water evaporates quickly"_
  - $Sprinkler$ = _"the sprinkler system is on"_
  - $Dew$ = _"there is morning dew"_

* Failure of Logic AI: Garden World Example

- Identify all exceptions and dependencies to make $X \implies Y$

  1. **Causal**: _"If it rains and there is no other source of water, grass will
  be wet"_
     - $Rain \land \lnot (Cover \land Evaporate ...) \implies WetGrass$

  2. **Exhaustive**: _"Grass is wet if it rains or sprinkler is on or morning
  dew"_
     - $WetGrass \implies Rain \lor Sprinkler \lor Dew \ldots$

```graphviz[width=80%]
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, penwidth=1.7];

    // Nodes
    Rain       [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    WetGrass   [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    Cover      [label="Cover", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Evaporate  [label="Evaporate", fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Sprinkler  [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#A0D6D1"];
    Dew        [label="Dew", fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];

    // Force ranks
    { rank=same; Cover; Evaporate; }
    { rank=same; Sprinkler; Dew; }

    // Edges
    Rain -> WetGrass;
    Rain -> Cover;
    Rain -> Evaporate;
    Cover -> WetGrass [label="blocks", style=dashed];
    Evaporate -> WetGrass [label="blocks", style=dashed];
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
    Dew -> WetGrass;
}
```

## Solution: Probabilistic Reasoning Under Uncertainty

### The Proper Approach

* Acting Under Uncertainty: Proper Solution

- @Conclusion@: We can't use propositional logic under uncertainty

- @Solution@: Acting under uncertainty requires combining:
  - **Probability**: handle uncertainty and partial knowledge
    - E.g., agent's internal estimate of outcome likelihoods
  - **Utility functions**: evaluate desirability of each outcome
    - E.g., combine goals like punctuality, comfort, legal compliance

- @Key idea@: **Rational choice** = plan that maximizes expected utility
  - Evaluate plans based on performance on average, given known info
  - Still success is not guaranteed

### Knowledge, Uncertainty, and Belief

* The Paradox of Probability and Knowledge

- @Paradox@: There is **no uncertainty in the actual world!**
  - E.g., the grass is wet, but either it rained or not

- @Answer@: **Knowledge is subjective**:
  - Probabilities relate to knowledge state, not to the real world
  - Updating knowledge changes probability statements

- @Example@
  - Initially, observe _"wet grass"_
    - From past data: $\Pr(Rain | WetGrass) = 0.8$
    - Current belief: _"80% chance it rained if grass is wet"_ (Bayesian prior)
  - Further observe _"sprinkler was on"_
    - Wet grass could be due to sprinkler, not rain
    - Belief changes: $\Pr(Rain | WetGrass \land Sprinkler) = 0.4$ (Bayesian
      update)
  - Learn from weather report that there was no rain
    - Belief changes: _"It didn't rain"_
    - Evidence overrides prior: $\Pr(Rain | WetGrass \land WeatherReport) = 0$
  - All statements were true even if contradictory
    - Need non-monotonic logic

# Probabilistic Reasoning

// From AIMA 13, Probabilistic reasoning (p. 425)

## Foundations: Representing Uncertainty

### Full Joint Probability Distribution

* Full Joint Probability Distribution

- @Definition@: Consider The **full joint probability distribution** of a set of
  random variables $X_1, X_2, \dots, X_n$ assigns probability to every possible
  world:
  $$
  \Pr(X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2, \dots, X_n = x_n)
  $$
  where _possible world_ is any assignment of values to all variables

- @Fact@: Full joint probability can answer any probabilistic query about domain

- @Cons@
  - Size grows exponentially $k^n$ with number of variables $n$ and values $k$
  - Impractical for real-world problems with many variables
  - Manually specifying each entry is tedious or impossible

- @Remark@: In the real world, many variables are **not fully dependent** from
  all others
- _Conditional and absolute independence_ of random variables:
  - Reduces number of variables needed in model
  - Makes compact and structured representations possible
    - E.g., factorized probabilistic models, Bayesian networks

### Independence of Random Variables

* Independence of Random Variables: Definition

- @Definition@: Two random variables $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** iff:
  $$
  \Pr(X, Y) = \Pr(X) \cdot \Pr(Y)
  $$

- _Equivalently_: Knowing $Y$ tells nothing about $X$
  $$
  \Pr(X | Y) = \Pr(X)
  $$
  - E.g., events "coin flip" and "weather" are independent
    $$
    \Pr(Coin=Heads | Weather=Rainy) = \Pr(Coin=Heads)
    $$

- @Consequence@: Independence of random variables:
  - Reduces number of parameters needed to model system
    $$
    \Pr(X_1 | X_2, X_3) = \Pr(X_1)
    $$
  - Allows factorization of joint distribution
    $$
    \Pr(X_1, X_2, X_3) = \Pr(X_1) \cdot \Pr(X_2) \cdot \Pr(X_3)
    $$

## Conditional Independence: Key Concept

### Definition and Basic Examples

* Conditional Independence: Definition

- @Definition@: Two random variables $X$ and $Y$ are **conditionally
  independent** given a random variable $Z$ iff knowing $Z$ makes $X$ and $Y$
  independent:
  $$
  X \perp Y | Z \iff \Pr(X, Y | Z) = \Pr(X | Z) \cdot \Pr(Y | Z)
  $$

- @Example@:
  - $X$ = _"it is raining today"_
  - $Y$ = _"a person is carrying an umbrella"_
  - $Z$ = _"the weather forecast"_
  - Without knowing $Z$: relationship between rain $X$ and umbrellas $Y$
  - Given/knowing $Z$: once forecast is known, knowing if someone carries
    umbrella $Y$ gives no info about rain $X$
  - Thus, $X$ and $Y$ are conditionally independent given $Z$

- @Pros@
  - Conditional independence more common than absolute independence
  - Simplify probabilistic models by factorizing joint conditional distribution
    into product of individual conditional distributions

* Conditional Independence: Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=65%}
- Two events can **become independent** once we know third event

- Consider:
  - $Fire$ = _"there is a fire in the house"_
  - $Toast$ = _"someone burned toast"_
  - $Alarm$ = _"the fire alarm rings"_
  - $Call$ = _"a friend calls to check on you"_

- **Dependencies**
  - $Alarm$ depends on $Fire$ or $Toast$
  - $Call$ depends on whether $Alarm$ rings

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Fire   [label="Fire",       fillcolor="#F4A6A6"];
    Toast  [label="Toast",      fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Alarm  [label="Alarm",      fillcolor="#A6E7F4"];
    Call   [label="Call",       fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];

    { rank = same; Fire; Toast; }
    { rank = same; Call; }

    Fire  -> Alarm;
    Toast -> Alarm;
    Alarm -> Call;
}
```
::::
:::

- **Conditional independence**
  - $Call$ is conditionally independent of $Fire$ and $Toast$ given $Alarm$
  - Once alarm rang, the specific cause doesn't affect whether friend calls
    $$\Pr(Call | Alarm, Fire, Toast) = \Pr(Call | Alarm)$$

- @Interpretation@: Knowing alarm rang "blocks" path of influence from $Fire$
  and
  $Toast$ to $Call$

### Conditional Independence in Garden World

* Conditional Independence: Garden World Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=70%}

- Garden world with $Rain$, $Sprinkler$, $WetGrass$
- Is $\Pr(Rain | Sprinkler) = \Pr(Rain)$?
  - No: if sprinkler is on, it's less likely it rained
  - $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are not independent

- Is $\Pr(Rain | Sprinkler, WetGrass) = \Pr(Rain | WetGrass)$?
  - Yes: knowing grass is wet, whether sprinkler was on tells you nothing more
    about rain
  - $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are conditionally independent given $WetGrass$

::::
:::: {.column width=30%}

```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Rain       [label="Rain",       fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Sprinkler  [label="Sprinkler",  fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    WetGrass   [label="WetGrass",   fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;

    // Added dotted arrows between Rain and Sprinkler
    Rain -> Sprinkler [style=dashed];
    Sprinkler -> Rain [style=dashed];
}
```
::::
:::

- @Interpretation@
  - Without $WetGrass$: $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ affect each other because both
    explain $WetGrass$
  - With $WetGrass$: once $WetGrass$ is observed, effect is "explained away"

- @Definition@: _"Explaining away"_ (collider) occurs when:
  - Two variables (causes) influence third variable (effect)
  - Observing effect creates dependence between causes
  - Evidence for one explains effect and reduces need to believe in other

## Bayesian Networks

### Core Concepts and Intuition

* Bayesian Networks: Definition

- Aka: "Bayes nets", "Belief networks", "Probabilistic networks", "Graphical
  models" (broader class), "Causal networks" (arrows have special meaning)

- @Definition@: A _Bayesian network_ is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG)
  1. _Nodes_ $X_i$ correspond to random variables (discrete or continuous)
  2. _Edges_ $X \to Y$ connect nodes and represent dependencies among variables
     - E.g., $X = Parent(Y)$, $Y = Child(X)$, ancestors, spouses, ...
  3. Each node $X_i$ associated with _conditional probability table_ (CPT or
  CPD): $$
     \Pr(X_i | Parents(X_i)) $$ quantifying effect of parents on node
     - If node has no parents, it has prior probability

* Bayesian Network: Intuition

- @Remark@: Bayesian networks analogous for uncertain knowledge to propositional
  logic for definite knowledge
  - Propositional logic = rigid rules (`True` or `False`)
  - Bayesian networks = flexible, degrees of belief
  - Replace $X \implies Y$ with $\Pr(Y | X)$

- @Example@: Garden world
  - $R$ = "It is raining", $W$ = "The grass is wet"
  - Propositional logic: If $R \rightarrow W$ and $R$ is true, then $W$ must be
    true
  - Bayesian network:
    $\Pr(R = True) = 0.2$, $\Pr(W | R) = 0.9$, $\Pr(W | \neg R) = 0.1$

- @Example@: Medical diagnosis
  - Propositional logic: _"Patient has disease $D$" $\implies$ "Patient has
    symptom $S$"_
  - Bayesian network: _"Probability of $S$ given $D$ is high, but not certain"_

* Bayesian Network and Full-joint Distribution

- @Theorem@: **Graph topology** and **conditional probabilities** are sufficient
  to specify any full joint distribution, (often) very concisely

- Topology of network (nodes and edges) specifies conditional independence
  relationships
  - E.g., $X \to Y$ means _"$X$ has direct influence on $Y$"_ (not necessarily
    _$X$ causes $Y$_)

- Nodes are:
  - Directly influenced by parents
  - Indirectly influenced by ancestors

- **How to build Bayesian Network**
  - Domain experts decide what relationships exist among variables, determining
    topology
  - Conditional probabilities can be specified or estimated

### Garden World: Understanding Independence Patterns

* Bayesian Networks: Garden World Example

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- Consider Garden World with 5 variables
  - $Rain$: directly influenced by $Weather$
  - $Sprinkler$: influenced by $Weather$ (e.g., less likely when raining)
  - $WetGrass$: affected by both $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$
  - $Weather$: general environmental conditions (e.g., sunny, cloudy)
  - $StockMarketUp$: whether stock market is up or down

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Weather       [label="Weather",       fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(S | W)"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];
    StockMarketUp [label="StockMarketUp", fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather   -> Rain;
    Weather   -> Sprinkler;
    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

* Garden World Example: Dependencies

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are **dependent** (marginally)
  $$Rain \not\perp Sprinkler \iff Rain \leftrightarrow Sprinkler$$
  - If no $Rain$, then $Weather = sunny$ and $Sprinkler$ more likely (sprinklers
    on in sunny weather)
  - $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ related since they share common parent $Weather$

- $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are **conditionally independent** given $Weather$
  $$Rain \perp Sprinkler | Weather$$
  - Once $Weather$ fixed, knowing if it rained tells nothing about $Sprinkler$
  - Correlation "explained" by $Weather$ (confounder)

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Weather       [label="Weather",       fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(S | W)"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];
    StockMarketUp [label="StockMarketUp", fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather   -> Rain;
    Weather   -> Sprinkler;
    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

* Garden World Example: Explaining Away

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are **conditionally independent** given $WetGrass$ (but
  only if $Weather$ not observed)
  $$Rain \perp Sprinkler | WetGrass \text{(but not } Weather)$$
  - If we condition on $WetGrass$ (grass is wet), knowing sprinkler was off
    increases chances it rained (and vice versa)

- $Rain$ and $Sprinkler$ are **conditionally dependent** given $WetGrass$ and
  $Weather$
  $$Rain \not\perp Sprinkler | WetGrass, Weather$$
  - If you know it's sunny, rain unlikely and sprinkler likely
  - If you also know $WetGrass$, learning $Sprinkler$ off $\implies$ must have
    $Rain$

- **Fork path** (common cause) and **collider path** (common effect)

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Weather       [label="Weather",       fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(S | W)"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];
    StockMarketUp [label="StockMarketUp", fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather   -> Rain;
    Weather   -> Sprinkler;
    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

* Garden World Example: Independence

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- $StockMarketUp$ is **(unconditionally) independent** of all other variables
  - But is it really?
  - Change in $StockMarket$ doesn't affect $Weather$
  - In the past, dependency of $StockMarket$ from $Weather$
    - Correlation between sunny weather in NYC and higher market index returns
    - Cold weather requires more heating oil

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Weather       [label="Weather",       fillcolor="#A6E7F4", xlabel="P(W)"];
    Rain [label="Rain", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(R | W)"];
    Sprinkler [label="Sprinkler", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(S | W)"];
    WetGrass [label="WetGrass", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(G | R, S)"];
    StockMarketUp [label="StockMarketUp", fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M)"];

    { rank = same; Rain; Sprinkler; }

    Weather   -> Rain;
    Weather   -> Sprinkler;
    Rain      -> WetGrass;
    Sprinkler -> WetGrass;
}
```
::::
:::

### Conditional Probability Tables

* Conditional Probability Table

- @Definition@: _Conditional Probability Table_ (CPT) encodes probability of
  node $X_i$ given parents $Parents(X_i)$
  $$
  \Pr(X_i | Parents(X_i))
  $$

- Each row of CPT contains conditional probability of node under conditioning
  case (possible combination of parent values)

- **Note**
  - Natural for discrete variables, but can extend to continuous variables
  - Conditional probability table summarizes infinite set of circumstances

* Conditional Probability Table: Example

- Consider events:
  - $T$ = "Traffic Jam", $S$ = "Snow", $R$ = "Rush Hour"
- CPT models $\Pr(T | S, R)$

\begingroup \scriptsize
| T 		| P(T\|S,R) | P(T\|S,-R) | P(T\|-S,R) | P(T\|-S,-R) |
|-------|----------|-----------|-----------|------------|
| True  | 0.80     | 0.10      | 0.50      | 0.05       |
| False | 0.20     | 0.90      | 0.50      | 0.95       |
\endgroup

- For any conditional probability:
  - Sum of probabilities on left side of conditioning is 1
  - Not on right side

- @Interpretation@
  - Traffic highly likely when both snow and rush hour
  - Traffic unlikely when neither condition holds

### Practical Application: Burglar Alarm Example

* Bayesian Networks: Burglar Example

- Famous example from Judea Pearl
- $Alarm$ system installed at home in LA
  - Detects $Burglary$, but also responds to minor $Earthquakes$
- Two neighbors, $John$ and $Mary$ will $Call$ when they hear $Alarm$

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- Structure of graph shows:
  - $Burglary$ and $Earthquake$ affect $Alarm$
  - $JohnCalls$ and $MaryCalls$ depend only on $Alarm$, not on $Burglary$ and
    $Earthquake$

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Burglary [label="Burglary", fillcolor="#A6C8F4"];
    Earthquake [label="Earthquake", fillcolor="#FFD1A6"];
    Alarm [label="Alarm", fillcolor="#B2E2B2"];
    JohnCalls [label="JohnCalls", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];
    MaryCalls [label="MaryCalls", fillcolor="#C6A6F4"];

    { rank = same; Burglary; Earthquake; }
    { rank = same; JohnCalls; MaryCalls; }

    Burglary -> Alarm;
    Earthquake -> Alarm;
    Alarm -> JohnCalls;
    Alarm -> MaryCalls;
}
```
::::
:::

* Burglar Example: Probabilities

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- Probability of:
  - $Burglary$ is 0.001
  - $Earthquake$ is 0.002

- $Alarm$ system:
  - Fairly reliable at detecting $Burglary$
  - Responds to minor $Earthquakes$ (false positives)

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Burglary** | **Earthquake** | **P(Alarm\| B,E)** |
| ------------ | -------------- | -------------------- |
| True         | True           | 0.95                 |
| True         | False          | 0.90                 |
| False        | True           | 0.30                 |
| False        | False          | 0.01                 |
\endgroup

- Events conditionally independent:
  $$\Pr(Alarm) = \Pr(Alarm | Burglary, Earthquake) \Pr(Burglary)
  \Pr(Earthquake)$$

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
```graphviz
digraph BayesianFlow {
    splines=true;
    nodesep=1.0;
    ranksep=0.75;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12,
    penwidth=1.4];

    Burglary [label="Burglary", fillcolor="#A6C8F4", xlabel="P(B) = 0.001"];
    Earthquake [label="Earthquake", fillcolor="#FFD1A6", xlabel="P(E) = 0.002"];
    Alarm [label="Alarm", fillcolor="#B2E2B2", xlabel="P(A | B,E)"];
    JohnCalls    [label="JohnCalls",    fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(J | A)"];
    MaryCalls    [label="MaryCalls",    fillcolor="#C6A6F4", xlabel="P(M | A)"];

    { rank = same; Burglary; Earthquake; }
    { rank = same; JohnCalls; MaryCalls; }

    Burglary   -> Alarm;
    Earthquake -> Alarm;
    Alarm      -> JohnCalls;
    Alarm      -> MaryCalls;
}
```
::::
:::

* Burglar Example: Neighbors

- Two neighbors, $John$ and $Mary$ will $Call$ when they hear $Alarm$

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- **John**
  - Almost always $Call$s when hears alarm
  - Sometimes confuses telephone with $Alarm$ and $Call$s (false positives)

- **Mary**
  - Misses alarm 30% of cases (false negatives)

::::
:::: {.column width=35%}
\vspace{-0.5cm}
\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Alarm** | **P(JohnCalls)** |
| --------- | --------------- |
| True      | 0.90            |
| False     | 0.05            |
\endgroup

\vspace{0.5cm}

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Alarm** | **P(MaryCalls)** |
| --------- | --------------- |
| True      | 0.70            |
| False     | 0.01            |
\endgroup
::::
:::

* Burglar Example: CPTs

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- Node without parents has unconditional probability

- Single input variable:
  - Sum of probabilities must be 1
  - Can remove redundancy

- Node with $k$ parents has $2^k$ possible rows

::::
:::: {.column width=45%}
\vspace{-1cm}
\begingroup \scriptsize
| **P(Burglary)** |
| --------------- |
| .001            |
\endgroup

\vspace{0.3cm}

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Alarm** | **P(JohnCalls)** | **P(-JohnCalls)** |
| --------- | --------------- | --------------- |
| True      | 0.90            | 0.10            |
| False     | 0.05            | 0.95            |
\endgroup

\vspace{0.3cm}

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Alarm** | **P(JohnCalls)** |
| --------- | --------------- |
| True      | 0.90            |
| False     | 0.05            |
\endgroup

\vspace{0.3cm}

\begingroup \scriptsize
| **Burglary** | **Earthquake** | **P(Alarm)** |
| ------------ | -------------- | ---------- |
| T            | T              | .95        |
| T            | F              | .94        |
| ...          | ...            | ...        |
\endgroup
::::
:::
